{"nodes": [{"id": "supabase_functions_fn_close_opening_balances_fn_close_opening_balances", "label": "fn_close_opening_balances", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "supabase/functions/fn_close_opening_balances.sql", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Card ^ref-62 T7 / ADR-021: closes opening balances one-way, permanently, for everyone. The completeness check and the close are deliberately the SAME operation (TDD Seam 1) \u2014 split apart, a row entered between check and close leaves a costless lot in an append-only ledger for ever with no path to fix it. The idempotency key is load-bearing rather than ceremonial: the same key returns the committed closed_at (R4 retry), a different key raises OPENING_ALREADY_CLOSED (TC-29); catching unique_violation and re-raising unconditionally would turn every dropped connection into a permanent-looking refusal on an operation the Owner cannot retry or undo. Completeness is a LEFT JOIN from stock_ledger OPENING rows to opening_costs, because the cost lives in a second table (the ledger refuses UPDATE), and the refusal names both the count and the lot codes so the Owner can act on it. R28 re-arms by consequence via fn_backdating_allowed, not by a second statement. Audit row comes from ^ref-06's generic trigger in the same transaction, never written here."}], "edges": [{"source": "supabase_functions_fn_close_opening_balances_fn_close_opening_balances", "target": "supabase_functions_fn_require_owner_fn_require_owner", "relation": "calls", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "supabase/functions/fn_close_opening_balances.sql", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "supabase_functions_fn_close_opening_balances_fn_close_opening_balances", "target": "supabase_migrations_20260910000012_opening_balances_opening_balance_close", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_f